    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1077">

                <pb facs="0915" n="799" xml:id="pg799"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.113/442.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Mexican Ambassador to the Secretary of
                    State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mexican Embassy</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Washington, D. C.</hi>
                        </placeName>
                        <lb/> [<date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content-and-sibling-dates"
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-04-20">undated</date>] (Received April
                        20, 1912—1.30 p.m.)</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>With reference to the note of this Embassy No. 1229 of 30th March,<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d1077-f1"/> your note 155, 6th April,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1077-f2"
                    /> and further correspondence relative to the exportation to Mexico of war
                    materials detained at Marfa, Texas, I have the honor to inform you that the
                    Consul of Mexico at El Paso has informed me that Marshal Nolte, who is detaining
                    said materials, says permission of the Department of Justice is required in
                    order to release said materials. Therefore I request that you be pleased to
                    direct what is necessary.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gilberto Crespo y Martinez</hi>.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
